From: dresden196 <50474030+dresden196@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] NickelOS: Exo keeps a cursor that didn't change

Exo makes the cursor again on every commit of a client's cursor surface:
a new bitmap, a new platform cursor, and a new hardware cursor image.
Waydroid's hwcomposer commits Android's pointer surface with every frame
Android draws, nearly always with the same image, so while the pointer was
over an Android window ChromeOS loaded a new hardware cursor every frame
(about 60 a second while scrolling) and the pointer lagged behind the
mouse. A commit whose image, hotspot and scale are the cursor's already
leaves it as it is; entering a surface still applies the cursor again.

diff --git a/components/exo/pointer.cc b/components/exo/pointer.cc
index 315110a03e13a..268f2b9a916e1 100644
--- a/components/exo/pointer.cc
+++ b/components/exo/pointer.cc
@@ -48,6 +48,7 @@
 #include "ui/gfx/geometry/transform_util.h"
 #include "ui/gfx/geometry/vector2d_conversions.h"
 #include "ui/gfx/geometry/vector2d_f.h"
+#include "ui/gfx/skia_util.h"
 #include "ui/ozone/public/input_controller.h"
 #include "ui/ozone/public/ozone_platform.h"
 #include "ui/views/widget/widget.h"
@@ -1055,6 +1056,18 @@ void Pointer::OnCursorBitmapObtained(const gfx::Point& hotspot,
     return;
   }
 
+  // NickelOS: some clients commit their cursor surface with every frame they
+  // draw, nearly always with the same image (Waydroid's Android does, on each
+  // frame while the pointer is over it). Making the cursor again for each one
+  // loads a new hardware cursor every frame, and the pointer lags behind the
+  // mouse. The cursor already shown stays when nothing about it changed
+  // (entering a surface applies it again).
+  if (cursor_ == ui::mojom::CursorType::kCustom && !cursor_bitmap_.isNull() &&
+      hotspot == cursor_hotspot_ && cursor_scale == cursor_scale_ &&
+      gfx::BitmapsAreEqual(cursor_bitmap, cursor_bitmap_)) {
+    return;
+  }
+
   cursor_bitmap_ = cursor_bitmap;
   cursor_scale_ = cursor_scale;
   DCHECK(cursor_bitmap_.readyToDraw());
